    "summary": "Fiery Half-Step opener, soaring Truckin’, and a smoking Wharf Rat",
    "blurb": "The show bursts open with a blistering Mississippi Half-Step Uptown Toodeloo and tight folk‑rock numbers like New Minglewood Blues and Tennessee Jed. Highlights include a monster Truckin’ finish, a scorching Wharf Rat jam, and strong performances from Jerry and the rhythm section. The setlist balances high‑energy peaks with a few uneven moments.",
    "review": "On November 6, 1977 at Broome County Arena, the Dead delivered one of the late‑'70s’s most exciting nights. An electrifying Mississippi Half-Step Uptown Toodeloo kicked off the first set, setting a high bar that was sustained through Jack Straw, Tennessee Jed and a crisp New Minglewood Blues. While Mexicali Blues and Passenger sagged a bit, the band rebounded with tight renditions of Friend Of The Devil, Dire Wolf and The Music Never Stopped.\n\nThe second set built momentum despite a sluggish Scarlet Begonias > Fire On The Mountain intro, exploding into a spectacular sequence: Good Lovin’, Saint Stephen → Drums → Not Fade Away → Wharf Rat → Saint Stephen → Truckin’. That chain produced one of the era’s most memorable Wharf Rat jams and a thunder‑ous Truckin’ that sent the crowd home on an exuberant Johnny B. Goode encore. Across all recordings, Jerry’s “wolf‑guitar” tone roared atop the mix, Phil laid down a frantic yet solid bass foundation, Bob’s vocal grit shone on New Minglewood Blues and Tennessee Jed, Keith supplied sparkling piano flourishes early on, and Bill (with Mickey’s complementary textures) kept the drums tight, especially during the St. Stephen > Drums transition.",
